Seminar: nature, life, perception, and intellect (@greek-seminar-nature-mind: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @greek-seminars, #source @greek-source-de-anima, #source @greek-source-physics, #source @greek-source-organon, #philosophy @greek-seminar-study, #ancient-greek-philosophy @greek-seminar-study) { r_2PYDKJQM81F9YKYZFJPEDNY150
**Preparation:** [Physics and kinds of explanation](../../aristotle/Nature-causes-and-being.lingua#r_2MGDMPK353BD8MFMFDMA3JPJ9P), [the earlier mind account](../../presocratics/Anaxagoras.lingua#r_2KMHNE6TG1NF6X3Q4SXMBHGWXN), and Greek textual analysis.

**Primary corpus:** read Physics II and [On the Soul](../../sources/On-the-Soul-Smith.lingua#r_3RK7WZ5X419B2RJGCK5E7K15TE) II–III as connected arguments, returning to Book I for the positions Aristotle examines. Use the Posterior Analytics for questions about explanation, definition, and the relation between a demonstrated fact and knowing why.

**Platonic alternative:** a project can read [Timaeus](https://classics.mit.edu/Plato/timaeus.html) as a whole through an [identified Greek edition](../../sources/Plato-critical-editions.lingua#r_28N1ADM4R3DAG5K2898WPKRXCK), investigating a plausible account of the world, mathematical order, necessity, intellect, and embodiment. State the mathematical preparation used by the interpretation. Compare a concrete explanatory problem with Aristotle while preserving each text's stated method and evidential aims.

**Aristotelian problems:** the relation of soul to a living body; how perception receives a form; how intellect differs from perception; or the role of ends in explaining living activity.

**Study requirements for either route:** use an appropriate critical Greek edition, its apparatus, identified translations, a substantial commentary, and competing scholarship. Choose a bounded dispute within the selected corpus.

Distinguish the work of a definition from an empirical observation, an explanation of a capacity, and a claim about its exercise. An interpretation of intellect in III.4–5 must account for its place in the surrounding treatment rather than treating a difficult paragraph as an isolated manifesto.

**Required work:** analyse the relevant Greek construction, reconstruct the complete local argument, compare two serious interpretations, and defend a reading of the author's explanatory claims. When comparing modern biology, psychology, or a model of cognition, supply the relevant evidence for the modern claim and state whether the accounts explain the same thing.

**Crossing:** a particular later commentator or account of mind can be studied through its actual text and language. Borrow the needed historical or linguistic preparation and return to the Greek question; reception is itself a claim requiring evidence.

**Review:** [Common seminar standard](../Sustained-study.lingua#r_7WN2AF9HW3X3BNQSTF5CKFJ22Q). **Return:** [Seminar families](Seminars.lingua#r_5FTSQDJTR924S9Z0G9EDBQFR1Y).
